// ============================================================
// leaky_io.kry -- COMPILE-FAIL fixture (expect-error).
//
// This file is SUPPOSED to fail `kryos check`. It is the negative half of the
// kryos-resilient-llm guarantee: the envelope is @capabilities(net), so its
// body provably touches the network and NOTHING else. A stray file_write in a
// net-only function is an attenuation error -- the secret/answer cannot be
// quietly written to disk.
//
// Expected outcome (NO --strict-capabilities needed -- the function is already
// annotated, so the checker enforces its declared ceiling):
//
//   kryos check fixtures/leaky_io.kry
//     -> error[E0505]: builtin `file_write` requires `io` capability
//     -> exit code 1
//
// Do NOT add `io` to the @capabilities annotation -- that would make it pass
// and defeat the fixture. The whole point is that a net-only envelope cannot
// reach the filesystem, and the compiler proves it before the program runs.
//
// This file lives under fixtures/ (NOT tests/) so `kryos test` does not try to
// compile it -- it is SUPPOSED to fail to compile. It is checked explicitly at
// the CLI and the E0505 output is pasted into the PR evidence.
// ============================================================

use std::llm::{LlmConfig, LlmResponse, Message, chat}

// A would-be resilient transport that leaks the model's reply to disk. The
// @capabilities(net) annotation is the envelope's promise; file_write breaks it.
@capabilities(net)
fn leaky_chat(cfg: LlmConfig, msgs: [Message]) -> str {
    let r = chat(cfg, msgs)
    // ---- the violation: a net-only function cannot touch the filesystem ----
    file_write("/tmp/leaked_answer.txt", r.text)
    return r.text
}

fn main() {
    println("this file must not compile")
}
